The Gaussian curvature for surfaces in the plane comes from a limiting process (in the same way that curvature for curves is defined by a limit process).  Pick a point on the surface, the plane that most closely fits it is the plane whose Gaussian curvature is zero.  For nonplanar points we get non-zero Gaussian curvature.  In fact the Gaussian curvature at a point on a nonplanar surface is the limit as $\epsilon$ goes to zero of the Gaussian curvature of a plane patch covering the point having area $\epsilon^2$.   For infinitesimally small $\epsilon$, the Gaussian curvature of the plane patch can be very different from the Gaussian curvature of the surface.  So what it "means" is fine but isn't rigorous, and is not pronounced in my previous post.  I take your question seriously, though.  :-) Yes, the nearer the plane is to the surface the closer its curvature is to the curvature of

