\fbox{[}13, 13+12\text{]}
\ldots\end{align*}$$
From here we notice that the intervals are actually overlapping. Moreover, the interval for the $n$th iteration (at the $n^{\text{th}}$ block of overlap) is exactly one 'off' by $n$. In other words, the shortest interval is completely covered, all other intervals are 1 'off', all intervals are 1 'off' and then one is 1 'off' and then they are all 'off', and so on.
It just so happens that 1 is completely covered and then covered by 1, then 1, then they are all 'off'. And when this block is done, the interval for that iteration are again 1 off, all intervals are 1 off and then they are all off. We can see a pattern now. The $n^{\text{th}}$ iteration always falls into $n+1$-th iteration.
